# M52 (PLAN.md 67.4): the marathon dry run's stand-in for player 1 -- humanlike.py
# ref/movies/board-start-com4.play 14000 34000 --seed 7 (the walk to the card, then a
# person to frame 34,000: long minigames and their results included), and START only at
# the instruction card: 14150 and 14200 (whichever the card takes; the other lands in the
# wipe, which ignores it).  M51's mg-human.play pressed START every 300 frames to 25,900,
# which pauses and unpauses a minigame -- and left Toad's Quick Draw paused for good.
# humanlike.py ref/movies/board-start-com4.play 14000 34000 --seed 7: the walk to frame 14000, then a person
# board-start-com4 -- board-start.play for a run whose four players are all CPU.
#
# Identical to board-start.play except that the four late single-frame STARTs
# (12968, 13224, 13480, 13736) are gone.  Those exist to accept the minigame
# instruction screen, and `--com4` already dismisses it from inside the game
# (src/REL/instDll/main.c:294), so under --com4 they have nothing to accept --
# what they actually do is open the board's own pause menu, whereupon the next
# beat of the A metronome (which runs to frame 29,960) walks into "Please
# choose which character's settings to change", exactly the trap
# board-start.play's own header describes, and the A metronome then holds it
# there forever.  A three-turn witness board lost thirteen minutes of game time
# to it before anyone looked at the screen; the watchdog did not call it stuck,
# because the cursor on that screen is alive.
#
# Use this script for any --com4 run.  board-start.play is still the right one
# for a walk with a human player in it.
#
# board-start -- past the board settings, through a turn of Toad's Midway
# Madness, and into the first minigame.
#
# The same walk as ref/movies/board-start.txt (the Dolphin side), rebased on
# where the port actually is: the console spends 186 frames on the cold-boot
# logos and 4,195 on the opening THP movie, and the port skips the movie
# outright and loads off a local disc image, so it arrives at the title around
# frame 700.
#
# The shape is what the two sides share:
#
#   START through the boot,
#   then A for four frames out of every sixty-four,
#   then single-frame STARTs, 256 apart, once the minigame instruction screen
#   is up.
#
# Everything except those last two is a metronome pinned to nothing, because no
# prompt in this run is at a frame either side can predict -- the new-file card
# scene's length moves everything after it.  The two STARTs are the exception
# and they have to be, for reasons that cost three captures:
#
#   * the minigame instruction screen ends on START and nothing else
#     (src/REL/instDll/main.c:293 tests `btnDown == PAD_BUTTON_START`; the
#     auto-start beside it needs all four players to be CPU and this walk
#     leaves one human), so A alone stops there forever;
#   * a START anywhere else opens the *board* pause menu
#     (src/game/board/pause.c:1493), which sleeps before it reads input, so the
#     next A walks into "Please choose which character's settings to change"
#     and the walk stays there.  A START metronome does this every 256 frames.
#     Both the port and Dolphin were stuck there, identically, which is its own
#     small piece of evidence.
#
# So the two taps are placed at frames read off an A-only capture, where the
# instruction screen is already up and waiting: the one pinned thing in the
# walk, pinned to an observation.  They sit 32 into the A metronome's period so
# they never share a frame with an A, which instDll's equality test would not
# match.
#
# 1P + 3 COM on EASY is what the character-select and board-settings defaults
# give, so no stick input is needed to set that up.

# Accept the title.
at 700 6 START
at 750 6 START
at 800 6 START
at 850 6 START
at 900 6 START
at 950 6 START
at 1000 6 START
at 1050 6 START
at 1100 6 START

